    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Louis I of Hungary" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_I_(Chronicon_Pictum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Márk Kálti, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ragusa, the Croatian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, grew up on a rocky islet off the southern Dalmatian coast; according to tradition it was founded in the seventh century by refugees from the Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavtat">Epidaurum</a> (modern Cavtat), destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, and a Slavic settlement grew up on the mainland opposite, the channel between them later being filled in. Through the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a Byzantine outpost, a self-governing commune that traded with its Slavic hinterland and defended itself against Arab and Venetian attacks. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> imposed its overlordship in 1205 and for a century and a half sent a Venetian count to preside over the city, while the commune kept its own councils and, from 1272, its own statute. In these years Ragusa acquired its first territories beyond the walls: the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lastovo">Lastovo</a>, the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mljet">Mljet</a>, and in 1333 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peljesac">Pelješac</a> peninsula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ston">Ston</a>, bought from the Serbian ruler. When Venice lost its war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zadar">Treaty of Zadar</a> of 1358 forced it to give up all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>. Ragusa accepted the distant suzerainty of the Hungarian crown, paying a modest annual tribute, but in practice became a self-governing aristocratic republic, free to choose its own officials and conduct its own trade.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/srebrenica.jpg" alt="Srebrenica" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Srebrenica_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jelle Visser, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ragusa lived by commerce. Its merchants carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and lead of the Serbian and Bosnian mines, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novo-brdo">Novo Brdo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a> and elsewhere, together with wax, hides, wool and slaves, from the Balkan interior to the coast, and returned with cloth, salt, wine and luxury goods. Ragusan colonies, with their own consuls, were established in towns across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, and caravans travelled routes into Ottoman lands that few other Christians could use. At sea, the republic’s merchant fleet grew in the sixteenth century into one of the largest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>; the English word “argosy” derives from the name of Ragusa. A commercial treatise of 1458 by the Ragusan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedikt-kotruljevic">Benedikt Kotruljević</a> contains one of the earliest descriptions of double-entry bookkeeping. Society was sharply divided: a closed nobility of some few dozen families held all political power, wealthy non-noble citizens organised in confraternities dominated commerce, and craftsmen, sailors and peasants formed the majority. The Catholic Church was central, and the city’s Franciscan pharmacy, recorded from 1317, still operates. Public welfare was notable for its time: an orphanage from 1432, a water supply built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onofrio-della-cava">Onofrio della Cava</a> in 1438, and quarantine regulations from 1377. The Great Council voted in 1416 to prohibit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantinople.jpg" alt="Constantinople" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantine_Constantinople-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cplakidas, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ragusa was too small to defend itself by force of arms, and its security depended mainly on diplomacy, tribute and its walls. The city walls, rebuilt and strengthened in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and the Ottoman advance, run for nearly two kilometres around the old town, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minceta-tower">Minčeta Tower</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-revelin">Fort Revelin</a> and the detached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-lovrijenac">Fort Lovrijenac</a> guarding the land and sea approaches; the long walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ston">Ston</a> protected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peljesac">Pelješac</a> peninsula and its salt pans. The republic kept a small militia and a few armed ships, and relied on hired soldiers when necessary. Its neutrality was its main defence: it paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, maintained good relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and avoided taking sides in the great wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the Ottomans, although Ragusan ships were sometimes requisitioned by Spain. Its principal danger came from Venice, which sought to dominate the Adriatic, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> of 1699 left two narrow strips of Ottoman territory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neum">Neum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutorina">Sutorina</a>, separating Ragusa from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetian-dalmatia">Venetian Dalmatia</a>. In 1806 French troops entered the city, which was then besieged by Russian and Montenegrin forces; the French broke the siege, but the republic’s independence was over.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/papacy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/papacy.jpg" alt="Papacy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Leo_XIV_3_(3x4_cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edgar Beltrán, The Pillar, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ragusa was governed by its nobility. Every adult male noble sat in the Great Council, which elected officials and passed laws, while the Senate, known as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consilium-rogatorum">Consilium Rogatorum</a></em>, directed foreign policy, finance and trade, and the Minor Council handled day-to-day administration. The head of state, the Rector, was elected for a single month and could not be re-elected for two years; during his term he lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rectors-palace-dubrovnik">Rector’s Palace</a> and could leave it only on official business. This rapid rotation, and a web of councils and magistracies checking one another, was designed to prevent any family or individual from gaining dominant power, and the republic never experienced the rule of a single lord. The statute of 1272, later supplemented by many decrees, regulated civil and criminal law, maritime affairs and commerce. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> was the language of official records, Italian of commerce and much correspondence, and Croatian the everyday language of the city. Ragusan diplomacy was skilful and active: the republic kept consuls in dozens of ports, sent envoys to the sultan each year with tribute, which was fixed at 12,500 ducats from 1481, and also accepted the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> until 1526 and maintained close relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The motto <em>Libertas</em> appeared on its flag.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dundo-maroje"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dundo-maroje.jpg" alt="Dundo Maroje" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dundo-maroje">Dundo Maroje</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dundo_Maroje_Zg_1008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roberta F., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ragusa was the principal intermediary between the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and its archive, one of the richest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, is a major source for the economic history of the Balkans from the thirteenth century onwards. It was also a centre of Croatian literature and learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marin-drzic">Marin Držić</a>, author of the comedy <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dundo-maroje">Dundo Maroje</a></em>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-gundulic">Ivan Gundulić</a>, whose epic <em>Osman</em> celebrated the Polish victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-khotyn-1621">Khotyn</a> in 1621, made Ragusan Croatian a literary language. The scientist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruder-boskovic">Ruđer Bošković</a>, born in Ragusa in 1711, became one of the leading natural philosophers of the eighteenth century. The republic’s quarantine regulations of 1377, requiring travellers from infected areas to spend a period in isolation outside the city, are often cited as among the earliest such measures in Europe, and its permanent lazaretto outside the walls served as a model. Ragusa’s survival as an independent republic between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the Ottomans for four and a half centuries made it a symbol of liberty for later Croatian national movements. The walled old town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, with its Renaissance and Baroque palaces, churches and fortifications, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and one of the most visited places in the Mediterranean.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars.jpg" alt="Napoleonic Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napoleonic_Wars_(revision).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ruedi33a, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ragusa’s decline was gradual. From the late sixteenth century the Atlantic shift of trade, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> competition and the decline of Balkan mining reduced its commercial base, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> itself was in relative decline. On 6 April 1667 a great earthquake destroyed much of the city and killed several thousand people, including the Rector, and fires completed the destruction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the Ottoman authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> tried to take advantage of the disaster, but the republic survived, rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style and found new security in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> in 1699. In the eighteenth century its neutral shipping revived and prospered during the wars between the great powers, with many ships trading across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and beyond. The French Revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> ended this position. In May 1806 French troops under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-lauriston">Jacques Lauriston</a> occupied Ragusa, ostensibly to protect it from the Russians, and the city was besieged by Russian and Montenegrin forces, which devastated its territory. On 31 January 1808 Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-de-marmont">Auguste Marmont</a> proclaimed the abolition of the republic, and its territory was soon incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 assigned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and the city is today part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of Ragusa was a small aristocratic merchant republic that kept its independence for 450 years between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> through trade, tribute and diplomacy, leaving behind a rich archive, a body of Croatian literature, early quarantine measures and the walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>.</em></p>

